gon Karthax's army closed in. the elf Luthien Silverleaf barred the door with a fallen pillar and grinned. 'Go,' they said. 'I'll hold them.' Rowan Ember refused to leave — so they held it together, back to back, as the dark came on."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Price', 'text': "the elf Luthien Silverleaf woke and wept to see it. 'The prophecy—' they began. Rowan Ember said: 'Prophecies are poems, not orders.' But the army of the dragon Karthax was coming, and they had no magic left.\n\nThey marched on the dragon Karthax's stronghold with the Crystal of Dawn dark but their hearts lit. Rowan Ember carried it anyway, empty as it was. Symbols, the elf Luthien Silverleaf said, have their own magic.\n\nThey escaped the fortress by griffon, the Crystal of Dawn burning like a small sun. But the elf Luthien Silverleaf was wounded, and the wound festered with dark magic. The healers said only the Crystal of Dawn itself could cure it — at the cost of its power."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Breaking of the Dark', 'text': "the Crystal of Dawn, dark and cold so long, blazed sudden white — fed not by magic but by the choice. The light struck the dragon Karthax's armor and the armor was empty; the dark lord was only a frightened soul inside, and the soul, seen, fled.\n\nThe battle for the Whispering Woods raged from dawn to dusk. the dragon Karthax rode out in black armor, terrible and splendid, and challenged Rowan Ember single combat. The armies held their breath.\n\nThen Rowan Ember remembered the seer's words and the dragon's riddle, and did the last thing the dragon Karthax expected: Rowan Ember offered mercy. 'Yield,' Rowan Ember said, 'and live.' the dragon Karthax laughed — and in that laugh, the dark wavered."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Return', 'text': 'Rowan Ember and the elf Luthien Silverleaf grew old telling the tale, arguing over every detail, and the Whispering Woods prospered. The prophecy, the elders admitted, had never named a chosen one at all. It had named a choice.\n